topics.referenceMarkdown:
ðŸ”´ Advanced Â· **Must-know**

*Client-side WebSocket API, `wss://`, and reconnection UX are in **Phase 3 Part 3 â†’ Web APIs**. This section is the server: accepting the connection, framing, heartbeats, and the state machine.*

**The handshake** â€” it is just an HTTP upgrade request, which is why it is easy to authenticate with cookies or headers and why proxies must be told not to buffer it:
```
GET /ws HTTP/1.1
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==
Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13

HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
Sec-WebSocket-Accept: s3pPLMBiTxaQ9kYGzzhZRbK+xOo=      // = base64(sha1(key + "258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-5AB0DC85B11"))
```
```ts
import { WebSocketServer, WebSocket } from 'ws';
const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true, maxPayload: 64 * 1024, perMessageDeflate: false });

server.on('upgrade', (req, socket, head) => {
  // authenticate + authorize BEFORE accepting, using normal HTTP pieces
  const url = new URL(req.url!, 'http://localhost');
  const session = verifySessionCookie(req.headers.cookie);   // or a signed token in the subprotocol
  if (!session || !url.searchParams.get('channel')) { socket.write('HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized\r\n\r\n'); socket.destroy(); return; }

  wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (ws) => {
    (ws as AuthedSocket).session = session;
    wss.emit('connection', ws, req);
  });
});

wss.on('connection', (ws: AuthedSocket, req) => {
  ws.isAlive = true;
  ws.on('pong', () => { ws.isAlive = true; });
  ws.on('message', (data, isBinary) => handle(ws, data, isBinary));
  ws.on('error', (err) => logger.warn({ err }, 'ws error'));
  ws.on('close', (code, reasonBuf) => logger.info({ code, reason: reasonBuf.toString() }, 'ws closed'));
});

// heartbeat: ping every 30s, terminate anything that missed two rounds
const ping = setInterval(() => {
  for (const ws of wss.clients as Set<AuthedSocket>) {
    if (!ws.isAlive) { logger.warn({ id: ws.id }, 'terminating unresponsive socket'); ws.terminate(); continue; }
    ws.isAlive = false;
    ws.ping();
  }
}, 30_000);
wss.on('close', () => clearInterval(ping));
```
- `perMessageDeflate: false` unless you have measured the need: compression costs CPU per message on the hot path and introduces the permessage-deflate CRIME-style attack surface with secrets in messages. Turn it on for text-heavy, large frames only.
- `maxPayload` is a hard DoS control. A frame larger than it is closed with code `1009`.

**Frame anatomy** (what a WebSocket library is doing for you)
```
byte 0:  FIN(1) RSV1-3(3)  opcode(4)
byte 1:  MASK(1)  payload-len(7)   [126 â†’ next 2 bytes = length; 127 â†’ next 8 bytes = length]
[4 bytes masking key, clientâ†’server frames only]
payload
```
| Opcode | Meaning | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `0x0` continuation | rest of a fragmented message | reassemble before dispatch |
| `0x1` text | UTF-8 text | serverâ†’client frames are NOT masked |
| `0x2` binary | bytes | decide the app framing yourself |
| `0x8` close | close handshake, carries a 2-byte code + reason | the polite way to end |
| `0x9` ping | liveness probe | serverâ†’client only |
| `0xA` pong | reply to ping | may also come unsolicited |

- **Masking** is mandatory clientâ†’server (proxies must not see your payloads) and forbidden serverâ†’client. A correct server must reject unmasked client frames (`1002` protocol error).
- **Control frames** must be â‰¤125 bytes, cannot be fragmented, and may be interleaved between fragments of a data message.
- **Fragmentation:** large messages arrive as `opcode 0x1, FIN=0`, then `0x0, FIN=0`, ... then `0x0, FIN=1`. A buggy or malicious peer can hold an incomplete message forever â†’ cap the reassembly buffer (this is why `maxPayload` matters).

**Close codes you must handle**
| Code | Name | Meaning to you |
|---|---|---|
| 1000 | normal | clean |
| 1001 | going away | server restart/deploy â†’ client should reconnect; make this deliberate |
| 1002 | protocol error | your client sent an invalid frame |
| 1008 | policy violation | auth failure or message too big |
| 1009 | message too big | `maxPayload` exceeded |
| 1011 | internal error | unexpected server failure |
| 1013 | try again later | server overloaded â€” use this instead of accepting then failing |
| 1015 | TLS handshake | reserved, must not be sent |
| 4000â€“4999 | application | private range: `4001 unauthorized`, `4003 rate-limited`, `4008 session-expired` |

**Connection state machine**
```
            TCP + HTTP upgrade 101
  CLOSED â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â–¶ OPEN
                                      â”‚  on('message') / on('ping') / send()
                                      â–¼
                                   ACTIVE â”€â”€â”€ send() while bufferedAmount > limit â”€â”€â–¶ BACKPRESSURED
                                      â”‚                                              â”‚  bufferedAmount drains
                                      â”‚  close() sent                                  â–¼
                                      â–¼                                            ACTIVE
                                  CLOSING â”€â”€â”€ peer replies close / socket ends â”€â”€â–¶ CLOSED
  CLOSED â—€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€ error, terminate(), TCP reset, or timeout â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”˜
```
```ts
// backpressure: the single most under-implemented part of a WebSocket server
const MAX_BUFFERED = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
function safeSend(ws: WebSocket, payload: string) {
  if (ws.readyState !== WebSocket.OPEN) return false;
  if (ws.bufferedAmount > MAX_BUFFERED) {
    logger.warn({ buffered: ws.bufferedAmount }, 'dropping message â€” slow consumer');
    ws.close(1013, 'client too slow');
    return false;                       // the alternative â€” buffering forever â€” is an OOM
  }
  ws.send(payload);
  return true;
}
```
- `ws.send()` with no callback does not tell you the kernel accepted the data. Use the callback to track per-socket write errors, and use `bufferedAmount` as the high-water mark for a slow consumer.
- Heartbeats must be enforced **server-side** with a deadline: ping every N seconds, terminate after two missed pongs. Relying on TCP keepalive alone is a mistake (default keepalive is 2 hours and many NATs drop idle mappings silently).

**Capacity planning numbers to know**
- Memory per connection is dominated by buffers: a typical Node WebSocket costs tens of KB. At 100k connections on one 16GB box you are memory-bound well before CPU.
- File descriptors: 1 per connection plus listeners. `ulimit -n` must exceed connection count; 100k connections needs `ulimit -n 200000`.
- The event loop is the real ceiling: heartbeats, sends, and per-message work are all on the same thread. Measure event-loop lag (see 3.3.6) â€” a 10ms p99 lag at 50k connections means you must shard onto more processes.
- Load balancer **idle timeout** must exceed your heartbeat interval (many LBs default to 60s) or the LB silently closes idle connections and every client sees a mysterious drop. And on deploy you must **drain**: stop accepting new upgrades, send `1001` (going away) or let heartbeats terminate, wait for connections to close, then exit â€” otherwise you cause a reconnect storm.

**Questions**
1. ðŸŸ¢ What is the `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` value and why must the server compute it?
2. ðŸŸ¢ Why must client frames be masked and server frames not?
3. ðŸŸ¡ What are the five opcodes and which must be â‰¤125 bytes?
4. ðŸŸ¡ How do you detect a dead-but-open connection, and why is TCP keepalive not enough?
5. ðŸŸ¡ What does `bufferedAmount` tell you and what is the correct response to a huge value?
6. ðŸŸ¡ Why send close code `1001` on deploy?
7. ðŸ”´ A client claims 4GB in a single frame. What protects you, and what happens if it is disabled?
8. ðŸ”´ Your LB closes connections every 60s and clients see random drops. Explain and fix.
9. ðŸ”´ Give the per-connection cost model (memory, FDs, CPU) and the resulting limits for one 16GB, 8-core Node process.
10. ðŸ”´ Explain fragmentation reassembly and the attack it enables.
11. ðŸ”´ What are the four drain steps for a zero-downtime WebSocket deploy?
12. âš« Why is `perMessageDeflate` often disabled server-side?

**Model answer, Q8:** "Context: WebSocket clients disconnect every 60â€“90 seconds at random, with no error on the client, and only some deployments show it. Decision: this is the classic load balancer idle-timeout problem, and the symptom pattern â€” roughly one minute, only on some paths, silent â€” is the fingerprint. I compare the LB's idle timeout with my heartbeat interval: an LB that closes at 60s and a server that pings only every 90s guarantees the LB wins. Fix: set the LB idle timeout above my heartbeat interval, and set my heartbeat interval comfortably below the LB timeout so *I* always know first. I send a WebSocket ping every 20â€“30 seconds and terminate anything that misses two rounds, so dead connections are detected in under a minute by me rather than being silently reaped by the edge. I also audit every hop â€” CDN, LBs between the CDN and origin â€” because there is often more than one idle timeout, and the innermost one is the one that matters. Trade-offs: heartbeats cost a small amount of bandwidth and CPU per connection, and aggressive timeouts will drop high-latency mobile clients that take 45s to wake; the mobile case argues for a somewhat longer deadline with a separate treatment for clients that are merely idle versus unresponsive. Failure scenario: a proxy that buffers rather than closing, so pings appear to succeed but nothing reaches the client â€” diagnosed by a packet capture or by testing through each hop individually. Prevention: an end-to-end synthetic WebSocket client on a 5-minute cycle through the real production path, which fails long before users report it."

**Mistakes:** not sending close codes; relying on TCP keepalive; ignoring `bufferedAmount`; not raising `ulimit -n`; deploying without drain; missing the LB idle-timeout interaction.

**Mini task:** build a `ws` server with token auth at upgrade time, a 30s ping/terminate heartbeat, `1009` handling via `maxPayload`, `bufferedAmount`-based dropping, and a drain-on-SIGTERM path; then use a local load tool to hold 20,000 connections and record memory, FDs, and event-loop lag.

